majordomo (0.4.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Reading as a Chat app (--as NAME, or identity= in a library call) names
    people: message and file senders, mentioned people and task assignees
    carry the display name the Chat API gives an app caller, where the name
    cache holds none. The app still has no People API and no Google Tasks
    list, so a person neither names shows as users/<id>.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 10:19:19 +1000

majordomo (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * majordomo imports as a Python library. load_config takes a dict in
    config.toml's shape or a path to a TOML file; make_reader, tasks and
    messages read with the credentials and name-cache directory they are
    handed; send posts; drain_notes returns what majordomo had to say.
    Importing the library has no side effect, and apart from your own
    config.toml, which load_config reads when handed nothing, it reads only
    what it is handed.
  * A refusal is raised as MajordomoError, with NotFound for a space,
    person or thread that matches nothing and ScopeError for credentials
    short of a Google scope, never as an exit. The command prints its one
    line and exits 1; an MCP client sees the same line.
  * --as NAME reads spaces, tasks and messages as the Chat app in
    [identity.NAME], over the Chat API and under the sieve: the spaces the
    app is a member of and their public messages. Reading messages as the
    app needs the chat.app.messages.readonly scope, which a Google
    Workspace administrator approves for the app once. --cache, --live,
    people, spaces --owner, attachments and login refuse under --as. The
    MCP spaces, tasks and messages tools take identity.
  * The cache database settings may sit in a [db] table in config.toml
    beside .env; a key set in both takes [db]'s value. WORLD_AS_OF is read
    by the command and the MCP server only; a library caller hands the
    bound to load_config.
  * A space named by id that does not exist, or that the sieve blocks,
    answers "not found" on every source, the cache included.
  * The command prints its notes on stderr after the up-front permission
    check, before a refusal's line, and at its end; the MCP server carries
    them in its answer.
  * A library call keeps the name cache in the cache_dir it is handed, or
    keeps none; the command and the MCP server keep it under
    $XDG_CACHE_HOME/majordomo/ as before.
  * tasks --to-me fills notes, due dates and titles from the person's own
    Google Tasks list, under the tasks.readonly permission, which opens a
    consent page once on upgrade.
  * Times print marked as UTC (+00:00), in the console and in JSON.
  * --live looks up a space name or DM the mirror lacks over the Chat API,
    and a cache miss says it looked only in the cache.
  * A missing database .env falls back to the direct API, as an absent
    driver does.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Sat, 17 Oct 2026 18:48:03 +1000

majordomo (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * send can post as a Chat app (a bot): name its service-account key as
    [identity.NAME] in config.toml, then run majordomo --as NAME send, or
    pass identity to the MCP send tool. The app sends text only, into
    spaces it is a member of, and --to reaches the app's own DM. Every
    other command reads as the logged-in account and refuses --as.
  * The MCP server runs on mcp 2; the mcp extra needs mcp 2.0.0 or later.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Mon, 12 Oct 2026 22:09:30 +1000

majordomo (0.2.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Reading the Chat API directly (--live, --nocache) replays each task's
    completion, re-opening, deletion and change of assignee from Chat's own
    messages, as the cache does.
  * attachments --download saves every file: several posted under one name
    (Chat posts pasted images all as "untitled") are written as untitled,
    untitled-2, untitled-3, and a file already in the directory is kept with
    the download saved beside it. A name without an extension takes one from
    the file type. A file that cannot be saved is reported on its row while
    the rest download, and the command exits 1.
  * python3-pymysql is recommended rather than required: it is needed only
    to read the server-side cache, and without it majordomo reads Google
    Chat directly.
  * The /majordomo command says how to write a message that someone will
    convert into a Chat task: the task in the first paragraph, under 135
    characters, the @-mention and details after a blank line.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Mon, 12 Oct 2026 17:13:51 +1000

majordomo (0.2.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * spaces reports whether each space belongs to a Google Workspace domain
    or was created from a personal account; spaces --owner names its owner.
  * People are named through the Google People API (profile, Workspace
    directory, contacts and other contacts): task assignees, message and
    attachment senders, space owners and the people report.
  * What majordomo learns about people and spaces is a refetchable cache,
    people.json and spaces.json under ~/.cache/majordomo/. The old
    known.tsv under ~/.local/state/majordomo/ is no longer read and can be
    deleted.
  * Upgrading needs new Google permissions (space memberships, contacts,
    other contacts, directory, basic profile). The first command that needs
    them opens Google's consent page once and then carries on; with nobody
    to answer, it shows ids and says why consent did not complete.
  * spaces --owner backs off and retries on Google's membership-read quota.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 23:54:33 +1000

majordomo (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Select by person: --person on messages and attachments, --assignee on
    tasks and --to on send take a users/<id>, an email, or a name seen in a
    task assignment or an @-mention. A name matches whole then substring
    and fails naming the candidates when several match. --person alone is
    your direct messages with that person, both sides; with --space, only
    their rows there.
  * Select a space by display name wherever --space is taken.
  * people rows carry every spelling a person has been called, their email
    and DM space where known, unwindowed; --person narrows to one.
  * What majordomo learns about people and spaces is kept in known.tsv
    under $XDG_STATE_HOME/majordomo (~/.local/state/majordomo when unset),
    written on every read and left untouched under WORLD_AS_OF.
  * --assignee-name (a glob) retires; --assignee takes a name.
  * A capped messages or attachments answer keeps the newest rows.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 20:45:36 +1000

